    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Despite the increase in the prevalence of suicide in adolescents over the past decades, early identification of adolescents at risk for developing suicidal ideation (SI) has not been successful. Previous studies have investigated potential psychobiological mechanisms underlying the development of SI; nevertheless, reliable predictors of SI have still not been identified. In addition, because the majority of studies focus on clinical samples, it is not clear how SI arises in the general adolescent population. This study aimed to address these gaps by investigating multimodal predictors of SI using longitudinal data obtained from a community sample of 105 adolescents across four timepoints. Specifically, self-reported measures of psychosocial functioning, diurnal and pubertal hormone assays, DNA samples, and measures of structural and functional brain connectivity obtained in early adolescence (T1 or T2) were used to predict SI in later adolescence (T3 or T4) using cross-validated least absolute shrinkage and selection operator logistic regression analyses. Among the 24 neurobiological and psychosocial predictors, 3 functional brain connectivity metrics, 1 hormonal measure, and 2 measures of psychosocial functioning significantly predicted subsequent SI. Overall, these findings underscore the major role of neurobiological factors in the development of adolescent SI and highlight the potential uses of these biomarkers to assess suicidal risk and inform targeted early interventions designed to mitigate these risks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
